            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "D Parker"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Good recipe. I am vegetarian so I used morningstar crumbles instead of meat and seafood. Cooking time is drastically reduced. If you use this method, you will want to put the crumbles in after the sauce and heat no more than 10 minutes. Also, I chopped the spinach in a food processor and I used olive oil instead of peanut oil. Yummy",
            "datePublished": "2009-12-29T21:32:03.553Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Sharon Carey"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I have a Nigerian friend who wanted me to make this for him. Needless to say, I was nervous, because he had the REAL THING! Not to worry, though... he said it was awesome! I put tripe in it instead of beef - slow-cooked the tripe for four hours on high. I put a little sriracha sauce in it to add a little kick...",
            "datePublished": "2018-02-21T00:23:08.850Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Keisha Butler-Ogunwuyi"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I did not like whole pumkin seeds in the soup when I made it the first time. I made changes to this recipe when I made it the second time by grinding the pumpkin seeds in a blender by itself with a little bit of water and one egg. I cooked the seeds separatly and then added it to the soup as the last step after the spinach cooked down. Speaking of spinach I found that the raw spinach is headache and time consuming. I used frozen chopped spinach and it was a great turn out.",
            "datePublished": "2009-12-12T17:56:30.607Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "sinfullysweet"
            },
            "reviewBody": "very good recipe, I use Egusi seeds and palm oil i have a African market about a hour away . also magi cubes cant live without magi .. I also boil my meat first using the water from the meat to make my stock . I love west African food which is good since my husband is Nigerian .",
            "datePublished": "2011-02-07T09:33:02.770Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Tiffany Knuckles"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I made with fish. Should have modified the recipe to add the fish closer to the end of cooking instead of at the start. Duh... but I was just so excited to start cooking, common sense was a few steps behind me. I reduced the amount of tomato soup based on other reviews and it seemed fine. Used maggi cubes and added ground shrimp. The melon probably could have been grounded a bit more. I blended them in a food processor, but there was still some graininess to the soup. Not terrible, just more than I would like. OVERALL great starter recipe for non-Nigerian folks like me who love the cuisine. Thanks !!",
            "datePublished": "2020-11-15T11:28:10.213Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "suzdchi"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Egusi Soup is the bomb; I make it without seafood and add fresh ginger in the blender with the habanero peppers and garlic; I tenderize my onion and add my sauce, then simmer my sauce until thick, then add my precook meat, ginger and garlic to sauce and serve with FuFu or rice and my friends beg for more; it is truly a great dish",
            "datePublished": "2010-02-27T15:28:43.340Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "TackyBread8885"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I've never cooked anything African before but this was great! I did go half on the chillies as I'm not that good with spice but definitely worth the effort!",
            "datePublished": "2024-05-13T22:23:39.306Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "sleepy1783"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I think this was a great basic recipe but I had to make a few changes. First, I cut the tomato sauce completely because I didn't want such a strong tomato taste. It's very important to grind the pumpkin seeds down VERY VERY fine, or else it will have a weird grainy texture. I added a beef bouillon cube to the pot, along with dried shrimp (If I had dried fish, I would have added that too). I also added a little bit of garlic salt, onion powder, and Italian seasoning. I also let the soup cook down for about 40 mins, and then I added more peanut oil to dry up the water. When it was finished I served over white rice. All and all I'd have to say that this recipe did the trick.",
            "datePublished": "2011-07-14T16:52:09.967Z"
          },
